From: MacFinals
Subject: ntdll: a call started after the scan's clock reads as a torn duration

The 2026-11-15 game run (macfinals-125) produced a false dump headline
("thread 03cc in NtSetEvent for over 60 s" - the thread was actually in
NtDelayExecution, 0.0 s; the game does ~1700 NtSetEvent/s) and 18446744073.7 s
body lines again. The scan and the dump read the clock once and then walk the
slots; a thread that enters a call in between has cur_start > now, the unsigned
subtraction wraps to ~2^64, and the result passes both the 60 s stuck filter and
the dur >= 0 print guard.

Treat cur_start > now as "just started": skip the slot in the scan, and fall back
to the no-duration body line in the dump.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
@@ -1079,7 +1079,10 @@
         int is_wait, known = 0;
 
         if (!tc->wtid || !key || !tc->cur_start) continue;
-        if (now - tc->cur_start < SYSCALL_LOG_STUCK_NS) continue;
+        /* cur_start > now: the thread entered this call after this scan read its clock;
+         * the unsigned subtraction would wrap to ~2^64 and pass the 60 s filter (a false
+         * "in NtSetEvent" headline seen in the 2026-11-15 game run) */
+        if (tc->cur_start > now || now - tc->cur_start < SYSCALL_LOG_STUCK_NS) continue;
         /* the name is the return value: syscall_log_name only fills the buffer for
          * unnamed calls */
         what = syscall_log_name( (key - 1) >> 12, (key - 1) & 0xfff, name, sizeof(name) );
@@ -1171,7 +1174,7 @@
                 if (i == nlive) fprintf( file, "  %04x exited\n", tc->wtid );
                 continue;
             }
-            dur = tc->cur_start ? (now - tc->cur_start) / 1e9 : -1;
+            dur = (tc->cur_start && tc->cur_start <= now) ? (now - tc->cur_start) / 1e9 : -1;
             if (dur >= 0)
                 fprintf( file, "  %04x (#%llx) in %s for %.1f s args=%lx,%lx,%lx,%lx\n",
                          tc->wtid, (unsigned long long)tc->tid, what, dur,
